1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,268
The brush clears a bull, standing on a lotus-shaped capital. It once crowned a pillar at Rampurva.

2
00:00:07,268 --> 00:00:14,578
It was raised by Ashoka, the Mauryan emperor. Early in his reign he was a conqueror. Later he gave up war.

3
00:00:14,578 --> 00:00:17,328
Why would a king give up war?

4
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:39,323
How do we know? From his own edicts, carved on rocks and pillars.

5
00:00:39,323 --> 00:00:44,032
Buddhist texts, including the Sri Lankan chronicles, add stories.

6
00:00:44,032 --> 00:00:49,314
The edicts are the more reliable source, since Ashoka spoke in his own voice.

7
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:12,947
Ashoka was the grandson of Chandragupta and the son of Bindusara. He ruled about 268 to 232 BCE.

8
00:01:12,947 --> 00:01:17,984
About 261 BCE, he conquered Kalinga, on the east coast.

9
00:01:17,984 --> 00:01:24,370
His thirteenth rock edict says that a hundred thousand people were killed, and a hundred and fifty thousand deported.

10
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:31,336
He expressed deep regret. After Kalinga, he turned toward dhamma. This was not a new religion.

11
00:01:31,336 --> 00:01:39,250
It was a code of conduct: respect for elders, kindness to servants, tolerance of all sects, and non-violence.

12
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:47,134
He appointed officers called dhamma mahamattas, to spread this policy. He also made journeys, called dhamma yatras.

13
00:01:47,134 --> 00:01:55,905
He cared for his people. He built roads, rest houses and wells, planted trees, and arranged care for the sick, and animals.

14
00:01:55,905 --> 00:02:03,592
He also sent missions to neighbouring lands. His son Mahendra and daughter Sanghamitra took Buddhism to Sri Lanka.

15
00:02:19,217 --> 00:02:25,278
Old name, new name. Ashoka's own edicts do not usually use his name.

16
00:02:25,278 --> 00:02:30,635
They call him Devanampiya Piyadasi, which means beloved of the gods, of gentle looks.

17
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:36,014
Only a few minor edicts, such as the one at Maski, name him Ashoka.

18
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:58,156
Carve this tip into memory. Kalinga, regret, dhamma. welfare, missions.

19
00:02:58,156 --> 00:03:02,837
Five words give the arc of Ashoka's reign, from war to welfare.

20
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:35,795
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which kingdom did Ashoka conquer about 261 BCE? The answer: Kalinga. Number two.

21
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:47,091
Name the pillar site where this bull capital was found. The answer: Rampurva. Number three.

22
00:03:47,091 --> 00:03:58,895
To which island did his children carry Buddhism? The answer: Sri Lanka. Now one line for Mains.

23
00:03:58,895 --> 00:04:04,231
Was Ashoka’s dhamma a religion or a code of conduct? Discuss.

24
00:04:23,850 --> 00:04:32,914
The bull capital goes into its case. It crowned a pillar at Rampurva. Three facts to take away. A bull on a lotus capital.

25
00:04:32,914 --> 00:04:42,274
From a pillar at Rampurva. Raised by Ashoka, about 250 BCE. Next gallery: Words in Stone.

